            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"The Holy Spirit, encompassing His indwelling presence, empowering, and illuminating grace.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Zechariah 4:1-6\",\"Matthew 25:1-13\",\"Exodus 27:20-21\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Inward spiritual readiness, genuine faith, or good works (especially charity and love).\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 25:1-13\",\"Luke 12:35\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Joy, gladness, and festive celebration.\",\"basis\":[\"Psalm 45:7\",\"Isaiah 61:3\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"The illuminating truth of the Word of God.\",\"basis\":[\"Psalm 119:105\",\"Proverbs 6:23\"],\"prominence\":\"rare\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"In Christian interpretation, lamp oil is overwhelmingly associated with the Holy Spirit, heavily influenced by Zechariah 4 where the oil supplying the lampstand is explicitly identified as God's Spirit. However, in the specific context of the Parable of the Ten Virgins (Matthew 25), exegetes have historically debated whether the oil strictly means the Holy Spirit, or broader concepts like inward grace, love (as argued by Augustine), or general spiritual preparedness.\"}",
